    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d154">

                <pb facs="0214" n="204" sameAs="frus:frus1945v07/pg204"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/1–2545: Airgram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hurley</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-02-07">February 7,
                        1945</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>A–31. Following is communicated for your information and, in your discretion, for
                    forwarding to John Service<anchor n="67" xml:id="d154-f1"/> as subject for
                    investigation and future report.</p>
                <p>Mao Tse-tung reportedly said in a speech of January 10 that there exists in the
                    “Communist” region “comrades” with different approaches to the problems of the
                    region. He characterized these differences as the “rural approach” and “urban
                    viewpoint”. Favoring the former himself, he comments that those who use the
                    urban viewpoint “produce many unsuitable plans”, ill-adopted to “deal with
                    problems encountered in political, military and economic work”.</p>
                <p>Mao’s statements raise the question of whether or not rival factions on basic
                    Communist policies exist or are developing in Communist circles.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d154-f1" n="67" xml:id="d154-f1-content">
                <p>Second Secretary of Embassy in China, on detail with U. S. Army Observer Section
                    at Yenan.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
